Historical Example: The Vanna Venturi House
A classic historical example of Postmodernism is the Vanna Venturi House in Chestnut Hill, Pennsylvania, designed by architect Robert Venturi for his mother between 1962 and 1964.
 Significance: It is widely considered one of the earliest and most definitive manifestations of Postmodern architecture.
 Characteristics: As a direct rebellion against the minimalist,
 functionalist "less is more" mantra of High Modernism (championed by architects like Mies van der Rohe), 
 Venturi’s design embraced complexity, contradiction, historical irony, and non-traditional symmetry. It features a broken gable roof, a monumental central split, oversized decorative lintels, and playful elements that mimic a child's drawing of a house while deliberately subverting traditional classical rules.
Source & Further Reading
 Primary Architectural Reference: Charles Jencks, The Language of Post-Modern Architecture (Academy Editions, 1977) — This foundational text formally defined the movement, documenting how architects shifted away from austere modernism toward pluralism, irony, and historical pastiche.
 Theoretical Context: Jean-François Lyotard, The Postmodern Condition: A Report on Knowledge (1979) — A key philosophical text that famously characterized postmodernism as an "incredulity toward metanarratives".
